1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,733
Even when shadows and reflections look
convincing, the final image does not consist

2
00:00:04,733 --> 00:00:09,067
entirely of freshly computed answers. This
episode connects virtual shadow

3
00:00:09,067 --> 00:00:13,400
maps and modern visibility processing to path
tracing, ray reconstruction,

4
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:18,133
and the different roles of DLSS. We finish
with a practical framework for inspecting

5
00:00:18,133 --> 00:00:22,800
the resources of a rendered frame. First,
where should shadow memory be allocated?

6
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,190
Earlier, a shadow map stored the closest depth
from a light's viewpoint.

7
00:00:27,190 --> 00:00:31,461
A visible surface was transformed into light
space and compared against that stored

8
00:00:31,461 --> 00:00:35,552
depth to test occlusion. Virtual shadow maps
retain this fundamental question.

9
00:00:35,552 --> 00:00:40,264
Allocating high resolution across an entire
large region is expensive.

10
00:00:40,264 --> 00:00:44,435
Detailed storage for areas that are tiny on
screen or completely

11
00:00:44,435 --> 00:00:48,142
invisible wastes memory and rendering work.
Meanwhile,

12
00:00:48,142 --> 00:00:52,312
a small shadow close to the camera may need
much denser sampling.

13
00:00:52,312 --> 00:00:57,441
Unreal Engine's virtual shadow maps divide a
large virtual address space into small pages.

14
00:00:57,441 --> 00:01:01,637
Pages needed by the current view receive
physical memory and are rendered.

15
00:01:01,637 --> 00:01:04,102
The size of the virtual address space must be

16
00:01:04,102 --> 00:01:07,032
distinguished from the memory actually
allocated.

17
00:01:07,032 --> 00:01:11,405
Consider an explanatory grid. A virtual shadow
region contains sixteen cells,

18
00:01:11,405 --> 00:01:16,103
but surfaces in the current view reference
only five. Allocating memory to those five

19
00:01:16,103 --> 00:01:20,932
illustrates the principle; these numbers are
not measurements from a particular game.

20
00:01:20,932 --> 00:01:25,654
Camera movement reveals different regions and
changes which pages are needed.

21
00:01:25,654 --> 00:01:30,657
If the light and objects remain unchanged,
previously rendered pages can be cached.

22
00:01:30,657 --> 00:01:36,012
Moving a light or changing a shadow-casting
object requires affected pages to be updated.

23
00:01:36,012 --> 00:01:39,472
Soft shadows require more than a single depth
comparison.

24
00:01:39,472 --> 00:01:44,414
Multiple samples estimate occlusion and the
penumbra associated with light size.

25
00:01:44,414 --> 00:01:47,309
Unreal's SMRT traces against shadow-map depth;

26
00:01:47,309 --> 00:01:52,462
it must be distinguished from tracing every
ray against the scene's actual triangles.

27
00:01:52,462 --> 00:01:57,763
Static scenes with effective caching can cost
differently from constantly changing scenes.

28
00:01:57,763 --> 00:02:02,929
A useful comparison examines newly rendered
pages, reused pages, and invalidated regions

29
00:02:02,929 --> 00:02:07,760
alongside resolution. This technique does not
solve global indirect illumination.

30
00:02:08,480 --> 00:02:12,727
Another strategy reduces the cost of
evaluating lighting and materials at high

31
00:02:12,727 --> 00:02:17,672
resolution. Temporal upscaling renders at a
lower internal resolution, then reconstructs

32
00:02:17,672 --> 00:02:22,172
a higher-resolution image using information
from the current and previous frames.

33
00:02:22,172 --> 00:02:27,355
Unreal's TSR and DLSS Super Resolution can be
compared in this role.

34
00:02:27,355 --> 00:02:31,174
Their implementations and requirements differ,

35
00:02:31,174 --> 00:02:36,629
but both require separating input resolution
from output resolution.

36
00:02:36,629 --> 00:02:43,812
A sharp image does not imply that lighting was
evaluated independently at every output pixel.

37
00:02:43,812 --> 00:02:48,766
Halving both width and height reduces internal
pixel count to one quarter.

38
00:02:48,766 --> 00:02:54,270
Frame time does not necessarily become one
quarter, because geometry processing,

39
00:02:54,270 --> 00:02:58,752
shadows, reconstruction itself, and other
fixed costs can remain.

40
00:02:58,752 --> 00:03:02,100
Motion vectors describe where previous samples
moved.

41
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:06,374
Camera projection, jitter conventions, and
object motion must agree.

42
00:03:06,374 --> 00:03:12,072
Incorrect vectors can retrieve history from a
different surface, producing ghosting or blur.

43
00:03:12,072 --> 00:03:16,747
Newly exposed regions may have no valid
history. Transparency, particles,

44
00:03:16,747 --> 00:03:21,130
thin structures, and interface graphics also
require consideration.

45
00:03:21,130 --> 00:03:26,242
Reconstruction depends on the quality and
validity of the available information.

46
00:03:26,242 --> 00:03:32,013
Introduced with DLSS 3 in 2022, frame
generation has a different role: it inserts

47
00:03:32,013 --> 00:03:38,038
display frames between rendered frames. An
increase in displayed frame count is not

48
00:03:38,038 --> 00:03:44,742
equivalent to the same proportional increase
in simulation or input-processing frequency.

49
00:03:44,742 --> 00:03:48,382
A useful comparison separates internal
resolution,

50
00:03:48,382 --> 00:03:52,263
rendered frame rate, displayed frame rate, and
latency.

51
00:03:52,263 --> 00:03:57,520
A single on-screen number can conflate
responsiveness with visual quality.

52
00:03:58,240 --> 00:04:02,999
Deferred rendering stores depth for position
reconstruction, normals, and material

53
00:04:02,999 --> 00:04:07,888
attributes in a G-buffer. A visibility buffer
makes a different storage choice: it first

54
00:04:07,888 --> 00:04:12,842
records identification information for the
triangle or instance visible at each sample.

55
00:04:12,842 --> 00:04:17,550
A later stage uses those identifiers to access
geometry. Vertex attributes are connected

56
00:04:17,550 --> 00:04:21,947
to the screen sample, and the required
material attributes and texture coordinates

57
00:04:21,947 --> 00:04:26,282
are reconstructed. The buffer stores identity,
not the finished lighting result.

58
00:04:26,282 --> 00:04:30,901
Suppose an illustrative screen sample
identifies triangle seven. Its three vertices

59
00:04:30,901 --> 00:04:35,520
are retrieved, and interpolation weights
produce the sample's texture coordinates.

60
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:40,392
A real implementation must correctly handle
perspective interpolation and derivatives.

61
00:04:40,392 --> 00:04:45,655
Derivatives used for texture mip selection
deserve particular attention. Reconstructing

62
00:04:45,655 --> 00:04:50,377
coordinates alone is insufficient. At a
boundary where adjacent samples identify

63
00:04:50,377 --> 00:04:55,032
different triangles, incorrect derivatives can
destabilize texture sampling.

64
00:04:55,032 --> 00:04:59,778
This approach can reduce bandwidth spent
writing and reading broad material attributes,

65
00:04:59,778 --> 00:05:04,024
but introduces geometry access and
reconstruction work. Results depend on data

66
00:05:04,024 --> 00:05:08,582
layout, material branching, caches,
compression, and multisampling requirements.

67
00:05:08,582 --> 00:05:13,635
Microsoft's official game-development sample
provides a concrete pipeline to study.

68
00:05:13,635 --> 00:05:19,098
Its publication date should not be interpreted
as the technique's invention date or universal

69
00:05:19,098 --> 00:05:24,560
adoption date. The comparison concerns stored
data and the responsibilities of later stages.

70
00:05:25,280 --> 00:05:30,941
Now connect tiled hardware with deferred
rendering. Tiled hardware can retain some

71
00:05:30,941 --> 00:05:36,362
data in nearby internal memory while
processing a small screen region. Deferred

72
00:05:36,362 --> 00:05:42,022
rendering concerns how material information
and lighting evaluation are ordered.

73
00:05:42,022 --> 00:05:44,394
These terms are not interchangeable.

74
00:05:44,394 --> 00:05:49,729
Tiled hardware can run forward or deferred
pipelines. A Forward+ screen-space light

75
00:05:49,729 --> 00:05:54,842
list is also not automatically the same
structure as the hardware's tile storage.

76
00:05:54,842 --> 00:05:59,439
On supported platforms and rendering paths,
Unreal's mobile deferred renderer

77
00:05:59,439 --> 00:06:03,629
can use tile memory for the G-buffer. The
relevant connection is reducing

78
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:08,022
the cost of writing intermediate data to
external memory and reading it back.

79
00:06:08,022 --> 00:06:12,810
Conditions depend on the graphics API, device,
available memory, and pass structure. It

80
00:06:12,810 --> 00:06:17,725
would be incorrect to claim that every mobile
deferred implementation avoids all external

81
00:06:17,725 --> 00:06:22,512
intermediate writes. Separated passes or
required stores can still consume bandwidth.

82
00:06:22,512 --> 00:06:26,170
Transparent surfaces can be handled through a
separate forward path.

83
00:06:26,170 --> 00:06:30,200
A G-buffer sample that records the frontmost
opaque surface does not contain

84
00:06:30,200 --> 00:06:33,982
enough information to describe multiple
transparent layers behind it.

85
00:06:33,982 --> 00:06:39,784
Forward and deferred should be evaluated
against materials, light counts, transparency,

86
00:06:39,784 --> 00:06:45,360
bandwidth, and device characteristics.
Multiple paths can coexist within one engine.

87
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:52,029
Cyberpunk 2077 patch 1.62 introduced Ray
Tracing: Overdrive as a technology preview

88
00:06:52,029 --> 00:06:57,812
in 2023. It provides a released-game example
for comparing path-traced lighting.

89
00:06:57,812 --> 00:07:02,852
Its requirements and preview status at the
time should remain explicit.

90
00:07:02,852 --> 00:07:06,449
Path tracing starts from a camera sample,
finds a surface, and samples subsequent

91
00:07:06,449 --> 00:07:10,151
directions according to the material.
Contributions along sampled paths estimate

92
00:07:10,151 --> 00:07:14,112
image brightness. This does not mean that
every possible path is exhaustively evaluated.

93
00:07:14,112 --> 00:07:18,037
A diffuse surface requires integrating light
arriving from many directions.

94
00:07:18,037 --> 00:07:22,022
A glossy surface may receive large
contributions near particular directions.

95
00:07:22,022 --> 00:07:24,638
Sampling important directions more often can
help,

96
00:07:24,638 --> 00:07:28,622
but contributions must be divided by the
corresponding sampling probability.

97
00:07:28,622 --> 00:07:31,959
For illustration, two directions contribute
two and eight.

98
00:07:31,959 --> 00:07:35,099
Equal-probability sampling targets an average
of five.

99
00:07:35,099 --> 00:07:39,874
A distribution that favors the brighter
direction must still estimate the same target

100
00:07:39,874 --> 00:07:44,322
after correct probability weighting. These
numbers are explanatory examples.

101
00:07:44,322 --> 00:07:47,443
Sampling a light directly and sampling a
material direction excel

102
00:07:47,443 --> 00:07:50,564
in different situations. Multiple importance
sampling combines

103
00:07:50,564 --> 00:07:53,963
strategies using their probabilities and
weights. Treating a sample as

104
00:07:53,963 --> 00:07:57,362
though it came from a different strategy
produces incorrect weighting.

105
00:07:57,362 --> 00:08:02,565
Extending paths increases cost. Russian
roulette probabilistically terminates some

106
00:08:02,565 --> 00:08:07,697
paths and compensates surviving contributions
by the inverse survival probability.

107
00:08:07,697 --> 00:08:09,806
With a survival probability of 0.8,

108
00:08:09,806 --> 00:08:13,672
a surviving contribution receives a factor of
one divided by 0.8.

109
00:08:13,672 --> 00:08:17,729
This compensation manages variance and cost.
It differs from always discarding

110
00:08:17,729 --> 00:08:21,725
seemingly unimportant paths. Maximum bounce
limits and other approximations

111
00:08:21,725 --> 00:08:25,602
require separate examination of an
implementation's bias and limitations.

112
00:08:25,602 --> 00:08:29,887
Error for independent samples is generally
related to the square root of sample count.

113
00:08:29,887 --> 00:08:33,937
Under comparable conditions, halving error
requires four times as many samples.

114
00:08:33,937 --> 00:08:36,988
Correlated temporal samples and reconstruction
algorithms

115
00:08:36,988 --> 00:08:40,392
prevent this simple relationship from
predicting the entire image.

116
00:08:40,392 --> 00:08:45,293
Understanding game path tracing requires more
than examining ray paths.

117
00:08:45,293 --> 00:08:50,747
Sample allocation, reuse, and reconstruction
of the remaining noise all matter.

118
00:08:50,747 --> 00:08:52,960
Reconstruction is our next step.

119
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:58,285
Low-sample ray results contain noise. A
denoiser tries to reduce it while

120
00:08:58,285 --> 00:09:02,592
preserving boundaries, material changes, and
reflection details.

121
00:09:02,592 --> 00:09:07,123
Simply averaging nearby colors removes genuine
detail as well as noise.

122
00:09:07,123 --> 00:09:12,063
Depth, normals, and material information help
determine which samples can be combined.

123
00:09:12,063 --> 00:09:16,674
History supplies additional evidence, but
values that no longer match the current

124
00:09:16,674 --> 00:09:21,483
surface must be rejected. Motion and newly
exposed regions complicate that decision.

125
00:09:21,483 --> 00:09:26,377
DLSS 3.5 introduced Ray Reconstruction in 2023
for reconstructing ray-based effects.

126
00:09:26,377 --> 00:09:30,874
It is a learned reconstruction feature
intended to replace multiple hand-tuned

127
00:09:30,874 --> 00:09:35,172
denoisers, and it depends on the inputs and
integration supplied by the game.

128
00:09:35,172 --> 00:09:39,444
Super Resolution, Frame Generation, and Ray
Reconstruction have different

129
00:09:39,444 --> 00:09:43,584
roles despite sharing a family name. One
reconstructs output resolution,

130
00:09:43,584 --> 00:09:47,724
another adds display frames, and another
reconstructs noisy ray results.

131
00:09:47,724 --> 00:09:50,813
A comparison must identify which features are
enabled.

132
00:09:50,813 --> 00:09:55,847
Useful comparison subjects include small
reflected text, moving objects,

133
00:09:55,847 --> 00:10:01,511
and thin lines. Inspect not just a still image
but how details stabilize after motion.

134
00:10:01,511 --> 00:10:04,973
Look for consistent detail, ghosting, and
flicker.

135
00:10:04,973 --> 00:10:10,565
A feature should not be declared universally
superior across all games and scenes. Matching

136
00:10:10,565 --> 00:10:16,013
camera, internal resolution, exposure, and
lighting makes differences interpretable.

137
00:10:16,013 --> 00:10:20,960
Promotional comparisons should also remain
distinct from direct measurements.

138
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:26,980
Unreal Engine 5.5 introduced experimental
MegaLights in 2024 as an approach to

139
00:10:26,980 --> 00:10:32,517
many dynamic lights and shadows. Its central
question is how to focus work on useful

140
00:10:32,517 --> 00:10:38,132
direct-light samples rather than evaluating
every light uniformly at every pixel.

141
00:10:38,132 --> 00:10:41,598
The system considers lights that can affect a
region and samples likely to

142
00:10:41,598 --> 00:10:45,175
contribute significantly. Selected samples
undergo visibility testing,

143
00:10:45,175 --> 00:10:47,131
and results are reused or reconstructed.

144
00:10:47,131 --> 00:10:51,603
Candidate selection and testing whether light
actually reaches a surface are separate tasks.

145
00:10:51,603 --> 00:10:56,501
Forward+ focused on narrowing light lists
before evaluating their contents.

146
00:10:56,501 --> 00:11:01,251
Stochastic selection also makes sampling
distributions, reuse, and noise

147
00:11:01,251 --> 00:11:06,373
handling important. Using many lights does not
make these approaches identical.

148
00:11:06,373 --> 00:11:10,329
Effective selection captures important
contributions with less work.

149
00:11:10,329 --> 00:11:15,194
Complex occlusion or rapidly changing bright
contributions can expose undersampling.

150
00:11:15,194 --> 00:11:17,788
Increasing light count should not be assumed
to

151
00:11:17,788 --> 00:11:20,512
leave both cost and quality perfectly
unchanged.

152
00:11:20,512 --> 00:11:23,046
MegaLights' direct lighting and Lumen's
indirect

153
00:11:23,046 --> 00:11:26,110
illumination connect through different
responsibilities.

154
00:11:26,110 --> 00:11:30,057
Enabling MegaLights does not automatically
make every indirect bounce exact.

155
00:11:30,057 --> 00:11:34,652
Engine version, supported paths, and feature
responsibilities need separate inspection.

156
00:11:34,652 --> 00:11:37,775
Experimental Nanite Foliage in Unreal 5.7,
introduced in 2025,

157
00:11:37,775 --> 00:11:41,534
provides a brief geometry example. It
addresses dense vegetation detail and

158
00:11:41,534 --> 00:11:45,872
representation across distance, rather than
naming a new indirect-lighting solution.

159
00:11:45,872 --> 00:11:49,920
Announcement status and actual project
integration must be recorded separately.

160
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:54,123
Depth testing can select the frontmost opaque
surface.

161
00:11:54,123 --> 00:11:59,385
Transparent surfaces also transmit
contributions from behind them. Glass, smoke,

162
00:11:59,385 --> 00:12:04,943
overlapping particles, and hair require more
information than a single stored surface.

163
00:12:04,943 --> 00:12:09,342
Conventional alpha compositing weights the
front color by alpha and the back

164
00:12:09,342 --> 00:12:13,538
color by one minus alpha. Alpha represents
opacity for compositing here.

165
00:12:13,538 --> 00:12:18,683
It should not automatically be equated with a
physical medium's absorption coefficient.

166
00:12:18,683 --> 00:12:22,116
Consider two half-opacity layers, red and
blue, over a black background.

167
00:12:22,116 --> 00:12:25,162
With red in front, red contributes 0.5 and
blue contributes 0.25.

168
00:12:25,162 --> 00:12:28,873
Reversing the order exchanges those
contributions. Order changes the result.

169
00:12:28,873 --> 00:12:32,697
Sorting only object centers cannot always
correctly order intersecting

170
00:12:32,697 --> 00:12:37,139
transparent surfaces. Their front-to-back
relationship can change across pixels.

171
00:12:37,139 --> 00:12:41,643
Object sorting and per-sample depth sorting
address different levels of the problem.

172
00:12:41,643 --> 00:12:45,773
Order-independent transparency encompasses
several approaches.

173
00:12:45,773 --> 00:12:50,759
Depth peeling can recover successive layers
but needs additional passes. Storing

174
00:12:50,759 --> 00:12:56,243
fragments in structures such as linked lists
introduces memory and data-structure costs.

175
00:12:56,243 --> 00:13:00,839
Weighted blending reduces sorting requirements
by choosing an approximation.

176
00:13:00,839 --> 00:13:04,827
It is not guaranteed to match exact
compositing of every sorted layer.

177
00:13:04,827 --> 00:13:10,303
Differences deserve inspection in strongly
overlapping, high-opacity, contrasting layers.

178
00:13:10,303 --> 00:13:15,059
Hair combines thin-strand coverage,
overlapping visibility, and directional

179
00:13:15,059 --> 00:13:19,746
scattering. Strand data, visibility, and
scattering models deserve separate

180
00:13:19,746 --> 00:13:24,853
examination. Handling hair does not make an
algorithm identical to volume rendering.

181
00:13:24,853 --> 00:13:29,303
Recent game-development presentations expose
production constraints. Their

182
00:13:29,303 --> 00:13:34,015
dates should not be labeled as the invention
dates of transparency or hair rendering.

183
00:13:34,015 --> 00:13:39,120
Our numerical examples also do not claim to
reproduce a game's undisclosed implementation.

184
00:13:39,840 --> 00:13:44,820
DLSS 4 Multi Frame Generation was introduced
in 2025 with support for adding

185
00:13:44,820 --> 00:13:49,878
up to three generated frames per rendered
frame under supported conditions.

186
00:13:49,878 --> 00:13:55,403
Its output is display frames; it does not
imply three additional sets of ray samples.

187
00:13:55,403 --> 00:14:00,795
In 2026, DLSS 4.5 expanded Super Resolution
and Multi Frame Generation features.

188
00:14:00,795 --> 00:14:06,032
Its 6X mode must be understood as up to five
generated frames per rendered frame.

189
00:14:06,032 --> 00:14:10,878
Dynamic operation considers display targets
and execution conditions;

190
00:14:10,878 --> 00:14:14,083
it does not promise 6X operation on every
device.

191
00:14:14,083 --> 00:14:19,290
DLSS 5 was announced in 2026 and became
available in NBA 2K27 in September.

192
00:14:19,290 --> 00:14:24,497
NVIDIA describes using game color, motion, and
3D guidance to improve the

193
00:14:24,497 --> 00:14:29,955
appearance of lighting and materials. We
discuss the vendor's input-output

194
00:14:29,955 --> 00:14:35,163
description without claiming our own quality
measurements of that game.

195
00:14:35,163 --> 00:14:39,628
Creators can use controls such as strength,
color treatment, and exclusions to preserve

196
00:14:39,628 --> 00:14:43,975
artistic intent. These controls connect the
feature's output to the intended design.

197
00:14:43,975 --> 00:14:48,143
They should be distinguished from exact
computation of every physical light path.

198
00:14:48,143 --> 00:14:52,256
Throughout this progression, actual
computation, temporal reconstruction,

199
00:14:52,256 --> 00:14:57,168
and display-frame generation should remain
distinct. Comparisons record hardware support,

200
00:14:57,168 --> 00:15:00,975
game version, internal resolution, and
latency. A higher version number

201
00:15:00,975 --> 00:15:04,720
does not mean every preceding function has
merged into one replacement.

202
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:09,685
Lighting results are not yet the colors sent
directly to a monitor.

203
00:15:09,685 --> 00:15:12,490
Bright values can exceed a display's range.

204
00:15:12,490 --> 00:15:17,493
Exposure and tone mapping connect that wider
range to the final display range.

205
00:15:17,493 --> 00:15:22,705
Exposure scales scene brightness, while tone
mapping applies nonlinear transformations

206
00:15:22,705 --> 00:15:27,646
such as highlight compression. Automatic
exposure can change the whole image without

207
00:15:27,646 --> 00:15:32,453
a lighting change, so technical comparisons
need controlled exposure conditions.

208
00:15:32,453 --> 00:15:36,701
Bloom adds the appearance of bright regions
spreading into neighboring pixels.

209
00:15:36,701 --> 00:15:40,449
A glowing image does not establish that
indirect illumination reached

210
00:15:40,449 --> 00:15:44,323
surrounding surfaces. Bloom around an edge is
not proof of a light bounce.

211
00:15:44,323 --> 00:15:47,314
Depth of field represents differences in focus
and distance,

212
00:15:47,314 --> 00:15:50,650
while motion blur approximates movement over
an exposure interval.

213
00:15:50,650 --> 00:15:54,964
These effects can conceal small shadow or
reflection errors or introduce their own blur.

214
00:15:54,964 --> 00:15:58,243
Comparisons should distinguish enabled and
disabled conditions.

215
00:15:58,243 --> 00:16:02,483
Check again whether color inputs represent
linear values or display values.

216
00:16:02,483 --> 00:16:06,984
This closes the linear-space discussion from
episode two. Missing or duplicated

217
00:16:06,984 --> 00:16:12,203
transformations at texture input, lighting,
and final output produce incorrect brightness.

218
00:16:12,203 --> 00:16:14,924
Final pixels combine geometry, visibility,
lighting,

219
00:16:14,924 --> 00:16:17,472
materials, reconstruction, and
post-processing.

220
00:16:17,472 --> 00:16:21,120
We can now examine how to trace those
connections during frame inspection.

221
00:16:21,840 --> 00:16:26,480
Feature names alone do not determine a frame's
actual structure. Engine version,

222
00:16:26,480 --> 00:16:30,985
platform, rendering path, and settings can
change its passes. A frame debugger

223
00:16:30,985 --> 00:16:35,423
such as RenderDoc helps inspect commands and
resources from a captured frame.

224
00:16:35,423 --> 00:16:40,772
Start with a comparison question: whether
reflections retain off-screen objects,

225
00:16:40,772 --> 00:16:45,143
how shadow pages are updated, or which
attributes a G-buffer stores.

226
00:16:45,143 --> 00:16:49,363
A specific question determines what to inspect
among many events.

227
00:16:49,363 --> 00:16:53,691
Record execution conditions: engine version,
graphics API, rendering path,

228
00:16:53,691 --> 00:16:57,232
internal and output resolution, camera, and
feature settings.

229
00:16:57,232 --> 00:17:01,823
Frames captured under differing conditions
cannot easily isolate a single cause.

230
00:17:01,823 --> 00:17:07,047
When opening a resource, inspect dimensions,
format, layers, and mip levels. Determine

231
00:17:07,047 --> 00:17:11,715
whether it is screen-sized, light-space data,
or a paged or layered structure.

232
00:17:11,715 --> 00:17:16,383
Names provide clues, but contents and usage
relationships need verification.

233
00:17:16,383 --> 00:17:20,469
Examine a depth resource using its depth
convention rather than treating it as

234
00:17:20,469 --> 00:17:24,617
a color image. Projected depth is generally
nonlinear with physical distance.

235
00:17:24,617 --> 00:17:29,012
Reversed-Z can also change which numerical
values represent near and far surfaces.

236
00:17:29,012 --> 00:17:33,088
Match G-buffer channels to engine
documentation. A normal buffer displayed as

237
00:17:33,088 --> 00:17:37,406
color is a visualization, not the surface's
actual color. Compression and encoding

238
00:17:37,406 --> 00:17:41,543
must be understood before interpreting channel
values as material attributes.

239
00:17:41,543 --> 00:17:46,156
For shadow resources, connect the producing
light with the consuming pass. Locate

240
00:17:46,156 --> 00:17:50,703
the transformation from a screen surface to
light space and its depth comparison.

241
00:17:50,703 --> 00:17:55,383
Cascades and pages require examination of
their partitioning and selection rules.

242
00:17:55,383 --> 00:18:00,402
In reflection passes, distinguish screen-depth
tracing from fallback inputs. Inspect

243
00:18:00,402 --> 00:18:05,421
whether probes or other trace results supply
values for off-screen or occluded regions.

244
00:18:05,421 --> 00:18:10,240
A single reflection image does not prove that
all samples were produced identically.

245
00:18:10,960 --> 00:18:14,972
Volume processing may require more than
viewing a color image.

246
00:18:14,972 --> 00:18:20,347
Separate spatial density and lighting grids
from view-integrated transmittance.

247
00:18:20,347 --> 00:18:25,193
Grid resolution and depth partitioning connect
to both artifacts and cost.

248
00:18:25,193 --> 00:18:30,212
Temporal processing separates history from
current inputs. Inspect which resources

249
00:18:30,212 --> 00:18:35,162
are written for later use and how motion and
depth reject samples. A single capture may

250
00:18:35,162 --> 00:18:40,113
omit earlier execution conditions, so its
interpretive limits need documentation.

251
00:18:40,113 --> 00:18:45,642
Compare color before and after tone mapping.
Locate exposure, color-space conversion,

252
00:18:45,642 --> 00:18:50,360
upscaling, and interface compositing in the
actual passes. Do not assume an

253
00:18:50,360 --> 00:18:55,373
identical order in every engine or label a
prepared diagram as a captured result.

254
00:18:55,373 --> 00:19:00,164
Selecting an event allows its input resources
to be traced back to their producers.

255
00:19:00,164 --> 00:19:04,613
Connecting producing and consuming commands
reveals a resource's role more

256
00:19:04,613 --> 00:19:09,473
concretely than a feature name. Resources can
also be reused for multiple purposes.

257
00:19:09,473 --> 00:19:13,618
Performance requires separate measurement.
Frame capture can introduce

258
00:19:13,618 --> 00:19:17,698
overhead or change execution behavior. Use the
engine's GPU profiler and

259
00:19:17,698 --> 00:19:22,173
repeated runs to inspect pass timing and
variability under matched conditions.

260
00:19:22,173 --> 00:19:26,103
Report sample count and variation alongside
averages.

261
00:19:26,103 --> 00:19:31,455
Check shader preparation, loading, cache
state, and matched camera paths.

262
00:19:31,455 --> 00:19:35,553
A single frame's timing may not represent an
entire scene.

263
00:19:35,553 --> 00:19:40,884
Record the connections among actual resources,
inputs, and outputs. Separating visual

264
00:19:40,884 --> 00:19:46,073
observations from implementation evidence
makes the scope of each conclusion clear.

265
00:19:46,073 --> 00:19:51,120
This method provides a more reusable
understanding than memorizing feature names.

266
00:19:51,840 --> 00:19:57,255
We connected DOOM's brightness values to baked
lighting, real-time shadows, screen-space

267
00:19:57,255 --> 00:20:02,393
effects, spatial grids, rays, caches, and
final reconstruction. This was not a history

268
00:20:02,393 --> 00:20:07,253
in which every older technique disappeared
simultaneously into one replacement.

269
00:20:07,253 --> 00:20:12,351
Our first question was what gets stored.
Brightness, surface attributes, depth,

270
00:20:12,351 --> 00:20:17,966
triangle identity, spatial lighting, and frame
history contain different information.

271
00:20:17,966 --> 00:20:22,473
Different storage determines which questions
later stages can answer.

272
00:20:22,473 --> 00:20:26,457
Our second question was where the renderer
searches: screen depth,

273
00:20:26,457 --> 00:20:31,420
a simplified spatial representation, or actual
triangles. Limitations involving

274
00:20:31,420 --> 00:20:36,383
off-screen objects, hidden surfaces, and thin
structures follow from this choice.

275
00:20:36,383 --> 00:20:40,901
Our third question was how much gets computed
and how results are reused.

276
00:20:40,901 --> 00:20:44,531
Light candidates, path samples, pages, caches,
history,

277
00:20:44,531 --> 00:20:47,865
and reconstruction have different costs and
errors.

278
00:20:47,865 --> 00:20:53,273
Timing changed alongside representation,
sampling, hardware, and reconstruction.

279
00:20:53,273 --> 00:20:57,875
When you next examine game lighting, think
beyond a feature name.

280
00:20:57,875 --> 00:21:01,892
Understanding the data and calculations behind
an image

281
00:21:01,892 --> 00:21:06,160
helps explain both why it looks convincing and
why it breaks.
